<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Maravi</h1><p class="st-meta">1480 CE – 1720 CE (approx.) · capital Mankhamba</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Chewa confederacy of the Kalonga at the southern end of Lake Malawi, which controlled the ivory trade of the lower Zambezi and gave Malawi its name.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to tradition, the Phiri clan migrated from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luba-empire">Luba</a> lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-basin">Congo basin</a> and settled south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-malawi">Lake Malawi</a> in the late 15th century. Their leader took the title Kalonga and set up his capital at Mankhamba, near the lake. Related chiefs spread out to found the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-undi">Kingdom of Undi</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lundu">Kingdom of Lundu</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Kalonga ruled a loose confederacy of chiefs bound by kinship and tribute. Wealth came from iron working, cloth and especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, sold to the Portuguese and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swahili">Swahili</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambezi">Zambezi</a>. Religion centred on the rain shrines of the high god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chisumphi">Chisumphi</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nyau">Nyau</a> masked societies. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalonga-masula">Kalonga Masula</a> in the early 17th century extended Maravi power towards the coast and in 1608 sent warriors to help the Portuguese support the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mutapa-empire">Mutapa</a> ruler against rebels.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalonga-masula">Masula</a>'s death the confederacy loosened, and by the early 18th century the Kalonga, Undi and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-lundu">Lundu</a> ruled separately. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngoni">Ngoni</a> and Yao incursions in the 19th century completed the break-up. The name Maravi survives in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malawi">Malawi</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-maravi.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Maravi" width="320" height="355"><figcaption>Image: Eric Gaba (Sting - fr:Sting); derivative work by moyogo · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maravi_Kingdom_map_c._1650s.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1480 CE – 1720 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mankhamba</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Kalonga Masula</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional Chewa religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Chichewa</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/malawi">Malawi</a>, <a href="/countries/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, <a href="/countries/zambia">Zambia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Maravi Confederacy, Malawi Empire, Kalonga</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1600&amp;focus=kingdom-of-maravi" aria-label="Kingdom of Maravi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5636 2736 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Maravi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5956 2992h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5956 2992h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1600&amp;focus=kingdom-of-maravi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
